{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2556 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "95fac76a9f2e3a42ade7c870f1e7b778c6d1095c193df164f502fe13d7728861" :edb/db-before-t 2555 :edb/db-after-t 2556 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535868 50 #inst "2026-10-09T09:24:07.615Z" 13194139535868 true] [17592186133410 1100 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=1$, and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z)\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k}$, express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." 13194139535868 false] [17592186133410 1100 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z )\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k},$ express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." 13194139535868 true] [17592186133410 1145 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R$, then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndivF &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + z) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2}y) \\\\\n&= 1 + z + 0 \\\\\n&= z + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)z + 1dV &= ∫_{-1}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}∫_{y}^{1 + x^{2} + y^{2}}z + 1dzdydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 false] [17592186133410 1145 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =1+z+0 \\\\  & =z+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,z+1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186133411 1100 "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}$. Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$." 13194139535868 false] [17592186133411 1100 "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186133411 1145 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R$, then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndivF &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\\n&= 2 + 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S$. Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∭_(R)3dV \\\\\n&= 3∭_(R)dV \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot Volume(R) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3}π \\cdot 2^{3} \\\\\n&= 32π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 false] [17592186133411 1145 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\  & =2+1+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186133412 1100 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=1$, $z=2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k}$, find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$." 13194139535868 false] [17592186133412 1100 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186133412 1145 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R$, then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndivF &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(e^{z}) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}y^{2}) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4x^{2}yz) \\\\\n&= 0 + 2x^{2}y + 4x^{2}y \\\\\n&= 6x^{2}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S$. Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∭_(R)6x^{2}ydV \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}∫_{1}^{2}6x^{2}ydzdydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}3x^{2}dx \\cdot ∫_{0}^{2}2ydy \\cdot ∫_{1}^{2}dz \\\\\n&= [x^{3}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\cdot [y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 2} \\cdot (2 - 1) \\\\\n&= (1 - 0) \\cdot (4 - 0) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 false] [17592186133412 1145 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2{x}^{2}y+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186133852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371404 13194139535868 true] [17592186133852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371411 13194139535868 true] [17592186133852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371418 13194139535868 true] [17592186133852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371425 13194139535868 true] [17592186133853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371376 13194139535868 true] [17592186133853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371383 13194139535868 true] [17592186133853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371390 13194139535868 true] [17592186133853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371397 13194139535868 true] [17592186133854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371432 13194139535868 true] [17592186133854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371439 13194139535868 true] [17592186133854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371446 13194139535868 true] [17592186133854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371453 13194139535868 true] [17592186134058 1017 #edb/ref 17592186130911 13194139535868 false] [17592186134058 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371370 13194139535868 true] [17592186134058 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535868 true] [17592186134058 1100 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y =-1$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = 1$, and the surfaces $x = y - 2z$ and $x = 4 - z^{2} - y^{2}$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F = xy^{2}i + xzj + yk$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS =$" 13194139535868 false] [17592186134058 1100 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186134058 1145 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186134059 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535868 true] [17592186134059 1100 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $x^{2} + (y - 2)^{2} + (z + 1)^{2} = 25$ and the vector field $F = xi + xzj + 3zk$. Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S$." 13194139535868 false] [17592186134059 1100 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=25$ and the vector field $F=x\\,i+xz\\,j+3z\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186134059 1145 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =1+0+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $5$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186134062 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535868 true] [17592186134062 1100 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x = 0$, $x = 2$, $y = 0$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = \\frac{π}{2}$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F = x^{2}\\cos zi + \\sin zj + (x + y)k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." 13194139535868 false] [17592186134062 1100 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,j+(x+y)\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186134062 1145 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+0+0 \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371371 13194139535868 true] [17592186371370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371372 13194139535868 true] [17592186371370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371373 13194139535868 true] [17592186371370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371374 13194139535868 true] [17592186371370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371375 13194139535868 true] [17592186371370 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535868 true] [17592186371370 1041 "selection" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371370 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371371 1319413953586