db1a677e92cac51954b0746cd083ab7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨x-y,\\,y+z,\\,2x-3z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371376 1113 #uuid "cccfe8f0-fae6-5eb5-97b7-607f248d5344" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371376 1145 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2x-3z) \\\\  & =1+1+(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}-1\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-\\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371377 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371378 13194139535868 true] [17592186371377 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371379 13194139535868 true] [17592186371377 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371380 13194139535868 true] [17592186371377 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371381 13194139535868 true] [17592186371377 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371382 13194139535868 true] [17592186371377 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535868 true] [17592186371377 1041 "selection" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371377 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371381 13194139535868 true] [17592186371377 1110 #uuid "ec78130e-b332-5bf6-9e83-6fc29a18b6ac" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371378 1034 "-16\\pi " 13194139535