,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=({x}^{2}+y)\\,i-yz\\,j+4xz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371411 1113 #uuid "20faa8ac-7475-55a0-94fb-35eb1571259e" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371411 1145 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4xz) \\\\  & =2x-z+4x \\\\  & =6x-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(6x-z)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,6x-z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371412 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371413 13194139535868 true] [17592186371412 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371414 13194139535868 true] [17592186371412 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371415 13194139535868 true] [17592186371412 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371416 13194139535868 true] [17592186371412 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371417 13194139535868 true] [17592186371412 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535868 true] [17592186371412 1041 "selection" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371412 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371415 13194139535868 true] [17592186371412 1110 #uuid "614dbe19-4218-5e05-8664-99c4d9231395" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371413 1034 "2x+3z" 13194139535868 true] [17592186371413 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13